"Chocolate Numbers (https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.06093)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.1 years ago; This paper enumerates the number of ways to break an $m \\times n$ bar, which we call chocolate numbers, and introduces four new sequences related to these numbers.\nTitle: Chocolate Numbers\nAuthors: Caleb Ji, Tanya Khovanova, Robin Park, Angela Song\nDate: Mon Sep 21 02:11:12 2015\n\nIn this paper, we consider a game played on a rectangular $m \\times n$ gridded chocolate bar. Each move, a player breaks the bar along a grid line. Each move after that consists of taking any piece of chocolate and breaking it again along existing grid lines, until just $mn$ individual squares remain. This paper enumerates the number of ways to break an $m \\times n$ bar, which we call chocolate numbers, and introduces four new sequences related to these numbers. Using various techniques, we prove interesting divisibility results regarding these sequences.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPerturbation Effects on Word Lengths in Three-Reflection Symmetric Presentations of Dihedral Groups (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392980260_Perturbation_Effects_on_Word_Lengths_in_Three-Reflection_Symmetric_Presentations_of_Dihedral_Groups)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; For a generating set S composed of three involutions, none of which belong ... In this paper, we will prove this conjecture and, more signiﬁcantly, introduce an alternative ... how 3-reﬂection presentations can be used to write elements of the Dihedral group with\n\nshorter word length than is possible for presentations containing two generators.\n\nfor all symmetric generating sets consisting of three reﬂections as follows.\n\nConjecture 1. For a generating set S composed of three involutions, none of which belong\n\nto the order n cyclic subgroup of D n, we conjecture that λ 1(D n, S)≤ ⌊n\n\n2⌋+ 1.\n\